    (0, None, '\'Max, we urgently need to clarify something.\' "Nam nuzhno", \'to us it\'s needed\', turns \'we need\' around, so the need happens to you. "Koye-chto", \'something\', is chto with koye- in front, for a particular thing the speaker has in mind.'),
    (1, None, '\'Guys, I really do not have time for you now.\' "Mne ne do vas", literally \'to me not up to you\', means \'I\'ve no time or patience for you\'. "Rebyata", \'guys\', is a friendly word for a group.'),
    (2, None, '\'What did you tell Nastya about me?\' "Nagovoril" adds na- to govorit\', and suggests saying a lot, usually nasty things about someone. "Naste" is Nastya in her \'to\' form.'),
    (3, None, '\'I was just joking a little, do not be mad.\' "Poshutil", \'joked\', comes from shutka, a joke, with po- for \'a little\'. Ne zlis\', \'don\'t be angry\', ends in -s\', short for -sya.'),
    (4, None, '\'Joking? You called me a real psycho!\' "Nazvat\'", to call someone something, puts the label in the \'with\' form: nastoyashchim man\'yakom. "Man\'yak" is the same word as \'maniac\'.'),
    (5, None, '\'I wanted to protect you from mistakes.\' "Uberech\'", to protect, is related to beregat\', to guard or look after. After ot, \'from\', oshibki becomes oshibok, the \'of\' plural.'),
    (6, None, '\'First you promised to help, and then bailed!\' "Kinul" is literally \'threw\', and in slang it means \'ditched\' or \'let down\'. "Snachala"... a potom is \'first... and then\'.'),
    (7, None, '\'I did not want to hurt you, Kostya.\' "Obidet\'", to hurt someone\'s feelings, is related to obida, an offence. "Khotel" ends in -l because Max is a man.'),
